\label{milnorsquare}
A \emph{Milnor square} of commutative rings is a diagram
\begin{equation}  \xymatrix{
S \ar[d] \ar[r]&  T \ar[d]  \\
S'  \ar[r] &  T'
} \label{milnsquare} \end{equation} 
such that the vertical arrows are surjective and such that the diagram is both
cartesian and cocartesian in the category of commutative rings. 
It follows in particular that 
if $I \subset S$, $J \subset T$
are the respective kernels of the vertical maps $S \to S'$, $T \to T'$, then $f|_I
: I \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} J$ establishes an isomorphism of the ideals $I$, $J$.


\label{milnsquarehens} 
Consider a Milnor square as in \eqref{milnsquare} with respect to the ideals $I
\subset S$, $J \subset T$. 
Then the henselizations $S^h$, $T^h$ of $S$, $T$ along $I$, $J$ fit into a Milnor square 
\[ \xymatrix{
S^h \ar[d]  \ar[r] &  T^h \ar[d]  \\
S' \ar[r] &  T'
}.\]
Furthermore, $T^h \simeq S^h \otimes_S T$.


